":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassander","c":2},{"y":-317,"t":"loss","x":"c. 317: Gandhara and the Punjab pass to Chandragupta. 'Chandragupta attacked the Greek-Indian governors during a period of unrest and local warfare after Alexander's death'; 'Jansari dates the arrival of Chandragupta in the Punjab at c. 317, in line with the chronology of Greek history'; 'Peithon withdrew to Arachosia…","cp":"maurya-empire","cpn":"Maurya Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chandragupta_Maurya","c":2},{"y":-316,"t":"contest_end","x":"Spring 316: Pydna falls; Olympias killed.","cp":"polyperchon","cpn":"Polyperchon's regency","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassander","c":3},{"y":-316,"t":"gain","x":"Spring 316: Cassander takes Macedon from the regent.","cp":"polyperchon","cpn":"Polyperchon's regency","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassander","c":2},{"y":-315,"t":"vassal_end","x":"315: Paeonia independent again under Audoleon.","cp":"kingdom-of-paeonia","cpn":"Kingdom of Paeonia","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paeonia_(kingdom)","c":2},{"y":-312,"t":"vassal_end","x":"312: Seleucus returns to Babylon as his own master.","cp":"seleucid-empire","cpn":"Seleucid Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_of_the_Diadochi","c":2},{"y":-312,"t":"loss","x":"312: Babylonia lost to Seleucus.","cp":"seleucid-empire","cpn":"Seleucid Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_of_the_Diadochi","c":2},{"y":-309,"t":"loss","x":"309: the east passes to Seleucus.","cp":"seleucid-empire","cpn":"Seleucid Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antigonus_I_Monophthalmus","c":2},{"y":-309,"t":"vassal_start","x":"309: Polyperchon becomes Cassander's man in the Peloponnese.","cp":"polyperchon","cpn":"Polyperchon's regency","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyperchon","c":2},{"y":-307,"t":"vassal_end","x":"307: Antipater's settlement of Greece ends.","cp":"greek-city-states","cpn":"Greek city-states (independent poleis)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_I_of_Macedon","c":2},{"y":-307,"t":"loss","x":"307: Demetrius takes Athens.","cp":"antigonid-dynasty","cpn":"Antigonid kingdom (Macedon)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_I_of_Macedon","c":3},{"y":-306,"t":"vassal_end","x":"306/305: Lysimachus king.","cp":"kingdom-of-lysimachus","cpn":"Kingdom of Lysimachus","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lysimachus","c":3},{"y":-306,"t":"split","x":"306: Antigonus king.","cp":"antigonid-dynasty","cpn":"Antigonid kingdom (Macedon)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antigonus_I_Monophthalmus","c":2},{"y":-305,"t":"rename","x":"305: Cassander proclaims himself king; the Antipatrid dynasty replaces the Argeads on the same land (rule 2: same colour).","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassander","c":3},{"y":-304,"t":"loss","x":"304-303: Demetrius 'returned a second time to Greece as liberator, and reinstated the Corinthian League'; 'In 304-303 he seized cities including Corinth'.","cp":"antigonid-dynasty","cpn":"Antigonid kingdom (Macedon)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_I_of_Macedon","c":2},{"y":-303,"t":"vassal_end","x":"303: Polyperchon's Peloponnese falls to Demetrius; he is last heard of after 304.","cp":"polyperchon","cpn":"Polyperchon's regency","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyperchon","c":2},{"y":-294,"t":"dissolve","x":"294: Demetrius Poliorcetes takes the throne of Macedon.","cp":"antigonid-dynasty","cpn":"Antigonid kingdom (Macedon)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_I_of_Macedon","c":3}],"wars":[{"name":"Philip II's wars of expansion (Third Sacred War and the northern conquests)","from":-358,"to":-346,"outcome":"Macedon takes the Thermaic and Strymon coasts, the Chalcidice and the Pangaion mines, becomes archon of Thessaly (352) and ends the Sacred War in 346 with the peace 'sworn in Thessaly'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_II_of_Macedon"},{"name":"Philip II's Thracian and Greek campaigns (Chaeronea)","from":-342,"to":-338,"outcome":"Thrace to the Hebrus annexed (342); the sieges of Perinthus (340) and Byzantium (339) 'both failed'; Chaeronea (338) gives Macedon the hegemony of the League of Corinth (337), not Greek territory.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_II_of_Macedon"},{"name":"Alexander's Balkan campaign","from":-335,"to":-335,"outcome":"The Triballi and the Illyrians defeated and the Danube reached; Thebes destroyed after its revolt.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_of_Alexander_the_Great"},{"name":"Wars of Alexander the Great","from":-334,"to":-323,"outcome":"Infobox 'Macedonian victory': the Achaemenid Empire conquered; the Macedonian Empire 'spans from the Balkans and Egypt in the west to Central Asia and India in the east'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_of_Alexander_the_Great"},{"name":"Agis III's war","from":-331,"to":-331,"outcome":"Macedonian victory: Antipater relieved Megalopolis and Agis III was killed in the battle; no land changed hands.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agis_III"},{"name":"Lamian War","from":-323,"to":-322,"outcome":"'Macedonian victory': Crannon 6 August 322, the Athenian fleet beaten off Amorgos; Athens takes a garrison and an oligarchy and loses Oropus and Samos; the Aetolians 'escaped unscathed'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamian_War"},{"name":"Wars of the Diadochi","from":-322,"to":-301,"outcome":"Infobox: First War (321-319) Antipatrid victory; Second (318-316) Antigonid-led coalition victory; Third (315-311) Antigonid defeat; Babylonian War (311-309) Seleucid victory; Fourth (307-301) Antigonid defeat.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_of_the_Diadochi"}],"certainty":[{"from":-675,"to":-294,"level":"soft","note":"Tribal and satrapal frontiers throughout: the Illyrian and Paeonian frontier in the north, the Thracian frontier on the Danube after 335, the satrapy boundaries of the Persian empire after 334 are all approximate."}],"sources":[{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonia_(ancient_kingdom)","title":"Macedonia (ancient kingdom